t      ],\n586\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n587\t    },\n588\t    {\n589\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n590\t        \"Shift from single-run SHAP point attribution to attribution plus per-instance reliability estimate (r_hat)\",\n591\t        \"Shift from naive diagonal jackknife (cycle 1, 0.976) to covariance-aware CRN/antithetic simulation (0.984) to partition ensemble with budget prior (0.985; sealed final ~0.984)\"\n592\t      ],\n593\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n594\t        \"Pima Indians Diabetes (OpenML 37)\",\n595\t        \"Statlog German Credit (UCI)\",\n596\t        \"Model families: logistic regression and other common tabular classifiers owned by the evaluator\"\n597\t      ],\n598\t      \"central_topic\": \"Whether a single run of a budget-limited stochastic Kernel SHAP explainer gives a reliable top-k feature attribution for a trained tabular model, and whether per-instance reliability can be predicted from that one run (within-run variance diagnostics) rather than from repeated runs.\",\n599\t      \"comparators\": [\n600\t        \"Improved/variance-reduced Kernel SHAP and regression-based Shapley estimators\",\n601\t        \"Sampling-based Shapley approximation with error guarantees (leverage score sampling)\",\n602\t        \"Explanation disagreement and instability literature (LIME/SHAP)\",\n603\t        \"Feature-selection stability and ranking-similarity measures (Nogueira; rank-biased overlap)\",\n604\t        \"Background-data and expectation-choice effects on SHAP (Yuan; Chen et al.)\",\n605\t        \"Uncertainty quantification for Shapley estimates\"\n606\t      ],\n607\t      \"core_\n608\t\n609\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n610\t```\n611\t\n612\t## manuscript source excerpt\n613\t\n614\t```tex\n615\t\\documentclass{article}\n616\t\n617\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n618\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n619\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n620\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n621\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n622\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n623\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n624\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n625\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n626\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n627\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n628\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n629\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n630\t}\n631\t\n632\t \n633\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n634\t\n635\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n636\t\\usepackage{url}\n637\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n638\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n639\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n640\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n641\t\\usepackage{array}\n642\t\n643\t\\newcommand{\\rhat}{\\hat r}\n644\t\\newcommand{\\topk}{\\mathrm{T}_3}\n645\t\\newcommand{\\jac}{\\mathrm{J}}\n646\t\n647\t\\title{How Reliable Is One Kernel SHAP Run? Budget-Conditional Top-3 Reproducibility and a Single-Run Self-Diagnostic}\n648\t\n649\t\\begin{document}\n650\t\\maketitle\n651\t\n652\t\\begin{abstract}\n653\tExplanations of tabular predictions are often produced by one run of a stochastic Shapley estimator, and the resulting top features are then reported as the reason for the prediction. We ask when the top-3 features of one budget-limited Kernel SHAP run would be reproduced by independent reruns. We define reproducibility as the mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between the assessed run and eight reruns, and we study coalition budgets of $4d$ and $16d$ for $d$ features. On three tabular datasets that are disjoint by record hash from the search data (one is the complementary half of a German Credit split used in the search, so it is not an independent source dataset) and three model families, mean agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$, so single-run reliability depends on the budget. We then test whether the assessed run's own coalition data can predict its reproducibility, using a jackknife-based rerun simulation shrunk toward a budget prior. In the sealed evaluation, which qualified, the predictor lowers squared error against always-reliable, constant-0.5 and split-half-gap baselines, with positive one-sided bootstrap lower bounds, and beats a pairing-permuted control by 0.0137. The permuted control keeps about 61\\% of the gain over always-reliable, per-instance rank correlation is 0.58, and the weights and shrinkage constants are hand-set and untuned. The budget-prior values are hand-set too: the configuration's notes describe them as aggregate label means from earlier evaluation metrics, but those means are not retained in the evidence, so their origin cannot be checked. The results are limited to Kernel SHAP, three datasets and two budgets.\n654\t\n655\t\\end{abstract}\n656\t\n657\t\\section{Introduction}\n658\tShapley-value attributions \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified} are used to explain individual predictions of tabular models in credit, medical and similar settings, and practitioner studies report feature attributions as a widely deployed form of explanation \\citep{Bhatt2020Explainable}. For models without a tractable exact algorithm, they are usually estimated by a stochastic procedure under a fixed budget of model evaluations. Two runs of the same estimator on the same trained model and the same instance therefore return different attribution vectors, and the features that appear in a short list of reasons can change from run to run. A common practice, which we take as the case of interest, is to run the explainer once and report what it returns. Whether that practice is adequate, and under which conditions, is rarely stated as a checkable claim.\n659\t\n660\tWe study a deliberately narrow version of this question. The explainer is Kernel SHAP with an interventional value function and a 32-row background, run with a budget of $B=4d$ or $B=16d$ coalitions for a model with $d$ features. The decision-relevant object is the top-3 feature set by absolute attribution. We call a single run \\emph{reproducible} to the degree that independent reruns, which differ only in their coalition sampling, return the same top-3 set. This definition ignores background resampling, model retraining and the choice between explainers; those sources of disagreement are outside our scope and we do not measure them.\n661\t\n662\tThe study has two parts. First, we measure how reproducible single runs are at the two budgets, on three tabular datasets and three model families. The datasets are disjoint by record hash from the search data used to select the diagnostic; one of them (German Credit) is the complementary half of a search-plane split, so that result does not come from an independent source dataset. Second, we ask whether reproducibility can be \\emph{predicted for a given run from that run alone}. A practitioner holds one run, not eight. A self-diagnostic that reads the run's coalition masks, weights and evaluated coalition values and returns a reproducibility estimate $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ would let the practitioner flag instances whose top-3 set should not be trusted. We evaluate such a diagnostic against three baselines and against a control that keeps all episode-level information but destroys instance-level pairing.\n663\t\n664\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n665\t\\begin{itemize}\n666\t\\item An operational, rerun-based reproducibility label for single-run top-3 attribution, together with a squared-error evaluation protocol that includes trivial baselines, a split-half-gap heuristic and a pairing-permuted control (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n667\t\\item A measurement of budget dependence. Across the final datasets and model families, mean top-3 Jaccard agreement is 0.807 at $4d$ and 0.932 at $16d$ (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n668\t\\item A single-run diagnostic that combines a delete-one-group jackknife of the run's own regression with Monte-Carlo propagation to the top-3 set and shrinkage toward a budget prior. It beats every baseline and the permuted control with positive lower bounds, but the margin over the control is small in absolute terms (0.0137), and most of its gain over the always-reliable baseline survives permutation.\n669\t\\item An explicit account of what is not established: no ablation of the diagnostic's components, no per-dataset or per-family breakdown in the retained evidence, only two budgets, and constants set by hand (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n670\t\\end{itemize}\n671\t\n672\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n673\t\\centering\n674\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n675\t\\caption{Protocol overview. A trained model and an explained instance yield one assessed Kernel SHAP run at budget $4d$ or $16d$. Eight independent reruns define the reproducibility label (mean top-3 Jaccard with the assessed run). The self-diagnostic sees only the assessed run and returns $\\rhat$, which is scored by squared error against the label and compared with three baselines and a pairing-permuted control.}\n676\t\\label{fig:overview}\n677\t\\end{figure}\n678\t\n679\t\\section{Related Work}\n680\t\\paragraph{Shapley estimation.}\n681\tKernel SHAP casts Shapley estimation as a weighted regression over sampled coalitions \\citep{Lundberg2017Unified}. Later work reduces its variance through paired sampling and regression refinements \\citep{Covert2020Improving}, permutation sampling \\citep{Mitchell2021Sampling}, leverage-score sampling with error guarantees \\citep{Musco2024Provably}, and amortized estimators \\citep{Jethani2021FastSHAP}. \\citet{Gupte2025Understanding} compare Shapley approximations empirically on tabular data. These papers make the estimator more accurate or give accuracy guarantees at a chosen budget. We take the budget as given and ask what a user holding one run can say about the reproducibility of its top-3 set, which is a statement about the run rather than about the estimator's average error. The closest work on sampled local explainers asks the per-explanation version of this question for LIME: \\citet{Visani2021Statistical} define stability indices from repeated runs, and \\citet{Slack2020Reliable} model the uncertainty of LIME and Kernel SHAP explanations with Bayesian local regressions. Both ask for more runs or a posterior over them. We instead ask whether the top-3 set returned by one run at a fixed budget will be reproduced by a rerun, using only that run's own coalition data and an evaluator that does reruns to supply the label.\n682\t\n683\t\\paragraph{What a Shapley value means.}\n684\tThe value function defines what is being attributed: interventional or conditional expectations answer different questions \\citep{Sundararajan2019many,Chen2020True,Frye2020Shapley,Kumar2020Problems,Covert2020Explaining}, and additive attributions can misrepresent models with interactions \\citep{Gosiewska2019Do}. We fix an interventional value function with a 32-row background and treat the resulting attribution, not its causal or data-faithful interpretation, as the object whose run-to-run stability is measured.\n685\t\n686\t\\paragraph{Instability and disagreement of explanations.}\n687\tLocal explanations are known to vary under perturbation and between methods \\citep{Ribeiro2016Why,AlvarezMelis2018Robustness,Krishna2022Disagreement}, and \\citet{Yuan2022empirical} study how background size affects SHAP stability for deep models. Our label isolates a different and narrower source of variation, namely the coalition sampling of a single estimator with everything else fixed. This is analogous in spirit to predictive multiplicity, which asks how much predictions vary across equally good alternatives \\citep{Marx2019Predictive}, except that the alternatives here are reruns of an estimator.\n688\t\n689\t\\paragraph{Stability and similarity measures.}\n690\tFeature-selection stability is commonly measured by average pairwise similarity of selected sets, with corrections for chance agreement \\citep{Nogueira2017Stability}, and rank-based comparisons of incomplete lists use measures such as rank-biased overlap \\citep{Webber2010similarity}. We use the plain Jaccard index on top-3 sets because three reasons is a natural unit for a decision explanation. We do not apply a chance correction, which matters when datasets with $d=10$ and $d=20$ are averaged (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}). Uncertainty quantification for Shapley-based importance has been developed at the population level \\citep{Williamson2020Efficient}; we address the per-instance, per-run level.\n691\t\n692\t\\paragraph{Tabular data and models.}\n693\tOur final datasets include an ACS income task from the folktables toolkit \\citep{Ding2021Retiring}. Our model families are logistic regression, random forest and a small MLP implemented in scikit-learn \\citep{Pedregosa2012Scikit}. Deep tabular architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021Revisiting} are not part of this study, and exact tree-specific Shapley algorithms \\citep{Lundberg2018Consistent} are not used. Our framing of reproducibility as a measurable property with explicit scope follows the call for rigorous evaluation of interpretability \\citep{DoshiVelez2017Towards}.\n694\t\n695\t\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n696\t\\subsection{Problem setup}\n697\tLet $f$ be a trained binary classifier and $f_1(x)$ its predicted probability of the positive class. Raw features are grouped so that a categorical feature, after one-hot encoding, forms one group; there are $d$ groups. For an explained instance $x$ and a background set of $m=32$ training rows, the interventional value of a coalition $S\\subseteq[d]$ is\n698\t\\begin{equation}\n699\tv(S)=\\frac1m\\sum_{b=1}^{m} f_1\\big(x_S,\\,b_{\\bar S}\\big),\n700\t\\end{equation}\n701\twith anchors $v_0=v(\\emptyset)$ and $v_1=v([d])=f_1(x)$. A Kernel SHAP \\emph{run} draws $B$ coalitions with masks $z_i\\in\\{0,1\\}^d$ and weights $w_i$ (summing to one), evaluates $v_i=v(S_i)$, and returns\n702\t\\begin{equation}\n703\t\\phi=\\arg\\min_{\\phi}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{B} w_i\\big(v_i-v_0-z_i^{\\top}\\phi\\big)^2\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad \\mathbf 1^{\\top}\\phi=v_1-v_0 .\n704\t\\end{equation}\n705\tThe coalition set follows the Kernel SHAP size distribution with complement pairs placed adjacently. Size classes are enumerated exhaustively when the budget affords it and the remainder is sampled in complement pairs; the weights of sampled coalitions are uniform over the remaining mass. The budget is $B=c\\,d$ with $c\\in\\{4,16\\}$, which gives $B\\in\\{40,160\\}$ for $d=10$ and $B\\in\\{80,320\\}$ for $d=20$ (evaluator source: \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluator\\_final.py}, \\texttt{kernel\\_coalitions}, lines 761--812).\n706\t\n707\tLet $\\topk(\\phi)$ be the indices of the three largest $|\\phi_j|$, and let $\\jac(A,B)=|A\\cap B|/|A\\cup B|$. For two top-3 sets with overlap $c\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}$ the Jaccard index is $c/(6-c)$, that is $0$, $0.2$, $0.5$ or $1$.\n708\t\n709\t\\paragraph{Reproducibility label.}\n710\tFor an assessed run $\\phi^{(0)}$ and $R=8$ reruns $\\phi^{(1)},\\dots,\\phi^{(R)}$ that use fresh coalition draws but the same model, instance, background and budget,\n711\t\\begin{equation}\n712\ty=\\frac1R\\sum_{r=1}^{R}\\jac\\big(\\topk(\\phi^{(0)}),\\,\\topk(\\phi^{(r)})\\big)\\in[0,1].\n713\t\\end{equation}\n714\tThe label is conditional on the particular assessed run, so it can in principle be predicted from that run's data. It measures coalition-sampling noise only. Because it uses $R=8$ reruns it is itself a noisy estimate of the conditional expectation, and we cannot quantify that noise from the retained evidence.\n715\t\n716\t\\paragraph{Diagnostic task.}\n717\tThe diagnostic receives, for each of up to 20 explained instances of a trained model, the assessed run's masks, weights, evaluated values and anchors, plus $d$, $B$, $k=3$ and the model-family name. It returns $\\rhat\\in[0,1]$ per instance. It never sees labels, reruns, model internals or dataset names.\n718\t\n719\t\\subsection{Design rationale}\n720\tKernel SHAP is a weighted regression, so run-to-run variation of $\\phi$ is sampling variation of regression coefficients. The top-3 set changes between runs when this variation is large relative to the gap between the third and fourth largest $|\\phi_j|$. A single run contains information about its own sampling variation: deleting random subsets of its coalition pairs and re-solving the regression shows how sensitive $\\phi$ is to the particular coalitions drawn. Propagating that sensitivity to the top-3 set by simulation gives an estimate of the expected overlap with a rerun, which is what the label measures. Two weaknesses motivate the remaining components. The jackknife is computed from only $B/2$ coalition pairs and is itself noisy, particularly at $4d$; and its spread need not match the rerun spread. We therefore shrink the simulated value toward a budget-specific prior. We hypothesized that this would reduce error most where the label is high and nearly constant (large budget) and that instance-specific information would matter most near ties between the third and fourth feature. We did not run component ablations, so these are design motivations and not tested mechanisms.\n721\t\n722\t\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n723\t\\paragraph{Self-diagnostic (frozen selected configuration).}\n724\tFor each instance, with $F=8$ folds and $P=4$ independent random partitions:\n725\t\\begin{enumerate}\n726\t\\item Assign complement pairs of coalitions at random to folds, drop one fold at a time, and re-solve the regression to obtain leave-fold-out solutions $\\phi^{(-f)}$. Folds that leave no more than $d$ rows are skipped. Define centered, scaled deviations $\\Delta_f=\\sqrt{(F'-1)/F'}\\,(\\phi^{(-f)}-\\bar\\phi^{(-\\cdot)})$ over the $F'$ valid folds, so that $\\sum_f\\Delta_f\\Delta_f^{\\top}$ is the delete-one-group jackknife covariance (the delete-a-group jackknife of survey sampling, \\citealp{Kott2001Using}).\n727\t\\item Simulate reruns with 1000 common-random-number draws (500 antithetic pairs, seeded by the episode seed). The diagonal simulator uses $\\phi+\\mu\\,\\sigma\\odot\\zeta$ with $\\sigma_j=(\\sum_f\\Delta_{fj}^2)^{1/2}$ and $\\zeta\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_d)$. The full-covariance simulator uses $\\phi+\\sum_f\\zeta_f\\Delta_f$, which preserves the efficiency constraint. Each simulated top-3 set is compared with the observed one by Jaccard index and averaged. This gives $s_{d,1}$ (diagonal, $\\mu=1$), $s_{d,0.7}$ (diagonal, $\\mu=0.7$) and $s_{f,1}$ (full covariance, $\\mu=1$).\n728\t\\item Compute the margin $z_{\\mathrm{m}}$, the gap between the third and fourth largest $|\\phi_j|$ divided by the jackknife standard error of that gap, averaged over partitions along with the three simulated values.\n729\t\\item Combine: $s=0.5\\,s_{d,1}+0.2\\,s_{d,0.7}+0.3\\,s_{f,1}$ and\n730\t\\begin{equation}\n731\t\\rhat=\\mathrm{clip}_{[0.02,1]}\\Big(0.6\\,s+0.4\\,\\pi(B/d)+0.02\\tanh\\big(z_{\\mathrm m}/3\\big)\\Big),\n732\t\\end{equation}\n733\twhere the prior $\\pi$ is linear in $\\log_2(B/d)$ between $0.84$ at $B=4d$ and $0.96$ at $B=16d$.\n734\t\\end{enumerate}\n735\tOn any numerical failure the output falls back to $\\pi(B/d)$. The source is in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_config\\_program.py} (constants at lines 35--46; functions \\texttt{\\_jackknife\\_cov}, \\texttt{\\_sim\\_jaccard}, \\texttt{\\_margin\\_z}, \\texttt{\\_empirical\\_bayes} and \\texttt{\\_diagnose\\_one}). The final record stores only a tree hash of the evaluated sources, so the above describes the source file of the configuration designated as selected in the search record, not a hash-verified copy.\n736\t\n737\tProvenance of the constants matters for interpretation. The weights $0.5/0.2/0.3$, $0.6$, $0.02$, the fold count and the partition count are hand-set and were not tuned on instance labels. The values $0.84$ and $0.96$ are hand-set constants (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_program.py}, lines 41--45); the configuration's notes describe them as aggregate label means by budget seen in earlier evaluation metrics (\\texttt{selected\\_config\\_NOTES.md}, ``Provenance of constants''), but no such means are retained in the evidence available to us, so we can neither verify the values nor their origin. The shrinkage weight is therefore a fixed constant, not an estimated empirical-Bayes weight; we use the term \\emph{empirical-Bayes-style} only for the structure of shrinking an unreliable per-unit estimate toward a prior derived from other data, as in Stein-type estimators \\citep{Efron1973Stein}.\n738\t\n739\t\\paragraph{Baselines and control.}\n740\tAll are scored with the same per-instance squared error $(\\rhat_i-y_i)^2$. \\emph{Always reliable} outputs $\\rhat=1$. \\emph{Constant 0.5} outputs $0.5$. The \\emph{split-half gap} heuristic is computed by the evaluator from the assessed run alone: it solves the regression separately on the two halves of the complement-pair blocks, sets $se_j=|\\phi^A_j-\\phi^B_j|/2$, forms $z=(|\\phi|_{(3)}-|\\phi|_{(4)})/\\sqrt{se_{(3)}^2+se_{(4)}^2}$, and outputs $0.5+0.5\\tanh z$. The \\emph{pairing-permuted control} scores the diagnostic's own outputs against labels after permuting them among the 20 instances of an episode with each of 20 fixed derangements and averaging. It therefore keeps everything the diagnostic knows about the episode, such as its budget, model and dataset level, and removes only the instance-level pairing between $\\rhat$ and $y$. The diagnostic exceeds this control only if its instance-specific variation is informative.\n741\t\n742\t\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n743\tThe evaluation was run once under a sealed protocol, with the selected configuration frozen beforehand. Datasets are the ACS income task (California, 2018, 1-year person records via folktables; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows), Give Me Some Credit (OpenML 46929; $d=10$; 20{,}000 sampled rows) and the second half of a hash-based split of Statlog German Credit ($d=20$; 485 rows). Models are scikit-learn logistic regression, a 100-tree random forest and a one-hidden-layer MLP with 64 units, trained on a 70\\% split with train-only median imputation and standardization. For each dataset, family, budget in $\\{4d,16d\\}$ and seed in $\\{9001,9002\\}$ there is one episode of 20 explained test instances, with nine runs per instance (one assessed, eight reruns). That gives 36 episodes and 720 labeled instances. The protocol constants in this paragraph (instances, reruns, derangements, bootstrap replicates, seeds) are read from \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluator\\_final.py} (line 42 for the seeds, lines 66--72 for the full-protocol values; lines 861--872 for the derangements) and \\texttt{task\\_contract.json}, not from the sealed metrics. Scores aggregate with equal weight over instances, then seeds within a dataset--family--budget cell, then cells within a dataset, then datasets. The headline quantity is $\\text{score}=1-\\text{MSE}$ of the diagnostic. A \\emph{skill} is the MSE of a comparator minus the MSE of the diagnostic. The evaluator reports one-sided 2.5th-percentile lower bounds from 200 hierarchical bootstrap replicates that resample cells, seeds and instances within each of the three fixed datasets. These datasets were disjoint, by normalized record hash, from the two search-plane datasets (a hash-based split of German Credit and Pima Diabetes) used to choose the configuration (\\texttt{final\\_resources\\_manifest.json}, \\texttt{search\\_resources\\_manifest.json}). The evaluator's pre-declared assessment treats the diagnostic as informative only if the three skill lower bounds are positive and its score exceeds the permuted control by a positive lower bound (\\texttt{task\\_contract.json}, \\texttt{protocol.promotion\\_rule}).\n744\t\n745\t\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n746\tAll numbers in this section come from the sealed final evaluation unless stated otherwise. The evaluation completed and its recorded outcome is \\emph{qualified}. The pre-declared assessment rule from the protocol is met by the reported lower bounds, all four of which are positive. This is a statement about the diagnostic's measured skill in this regime, and not a validity, superiority or generality claim about Kernel SHAP reliability.\n747\t\n748\t\\subsection{Single-run reproducibility depends on the budget}\n749\tMean top-3 Jaccard agreement is 0.8065 at $B=4d$ and 0.9315 at $B=16d$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b). \n750\tThe difference is 0.125. Since a one-feature swap gives Jaccard 0.5 and larger disagreements give at most 0.2, the loss $1-\\bar y$ bounds the share of assessed--rerun pairs with non-identical top-3 sets. That share is at least $1-\\bar y$ and at most $2(1-\\bar y)$: between 19.3\\% and 38.7\\% at $4d$, and between 6.8\\% and 13.7\\% at $16d$ (derived; \\texttt{analysis/derived\\_metrics.json}). \n751\tEven at $16d$, then, a noticeable minority of rerun pairs disagree on the identity of the top three features, and at $4d$ a substantial minority do. These are averages over datasets, model families, seeds and instances. The retained final evidence contains only per-seed aggregate scores, not per-cell labels, so we cannot say which datasets, families or instances drive them.\n752\t\n753\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n754\t\\centering\n755\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_comparison.pdf}\n756\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation. (a) Reduction in mean squared error of the single-run diagnostic relative to each baseline and to the pairing-permuted control, with the evaluator's one-sided 2.5th-percentile bootstrap lower bound (diamonds). (b) Mean top-3 Jaccard agreement between an assessed run and eight reruns at the two budgets, averaged over three final datasets, three model families, two seeds and 20 instances per cell.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9c0c424-1d9c-4069-a23f-da5c5283f4a7","uuid":"5844cd19-361a-4bf